      "side": 1,
      "message": "Mhm, if a caller keeps the reference from `context()` alive across an evaluation, this block hands out `\u0026mut FakeEvalContext` to the same object while that shared `\u0026` is still live.\n\nThe current `\u0026self` signatures on both `context()` and the eval methods allow exactly that overlap, and it becomes UB as soon as evaluated code touches `context_mut` (e.g. `require_rule_impl_mut`).\n\nSequential use (inspect only after eval returns) is safe, so nothing breaks today. But should we enforce real exclusivity instead - interior mutability with runtime borrow checking (`RefCell`), or requiring `\u0026mut` for evaluation?",
      "revId": "3bc39ffe71f1584c5bc2feaa951d8f83d2b48cbd",
